\cf0 If any of these scripts fail to work on your computer, add a 'delay 1' statement in strategic places where the script might get ahead of the target application. If that doesn't work, try making the delay longer. Sometimes, a delay of 10 seconds or more can make the difference on a slower machine.\
\
For other examples of GUI Scripting scripts, look in the local Library's Scripts file on your computer, under UI Element Scripts.\
\
These scripts do not require UI Browser to run, but you may find UI Browser very helpful if you try to modify them.\
\

\b Clock Menu Extra - View as Analog
\b0 \
\
Menu extras are scriptable using GUI Scripting. This script demonstrates how to execute menu extra commands. It uses GUI Scripting to set the Clock menu extra to display an analog clock.\
\
Menu Extras are the menu bar items that appear at the right end of the menu bar. They are owned by the SystemUIServer application, which has its own menu bar.\
\

\b iTunes Dock Item - Set Repeat All
\b0 \
\
Dock menus are scriptable using GUI Scripting. This script demonstrates how to determine whether a specific Dock item is present and whether its application is running and, if so, to execute its Dock menu commands. It uses GUI Scripting to turn on the Repeat All setting in iTunes. iTunes must be running and your Music library must be selected.\
\

\b Restart Choosing Disk
\b0 \
\
The buttons in the Startup Items pane of System Preferences are scriptable using GUI Scripting. This script demonstrates how to restart the computer using a startup disk selected from a list of available disks. It uses GUI Scripting to list all available local disks and lets the user select the new startup disk by name from the list. It takes advantage of the fact that button names of local disks begin with "Mac OS" and end with the disk name
